o  it properly claim number two the keto  diet can help treat autism let's meet  our friend Aaron  we knew from the moment he was put in  our arms at two weeks old we knew  something was amiss when he was three I  decided to put him on a specific  carbohydrate diet just meat vegetable  fruit nuts and seeds third day on  specific carbohydrate diet he fed  himself for the first time the fifth day  he said his first word so Aaron is  obviously on the autism spectrum and  after six months of being on the  ketogenic diet  apparently he's able to express himself  and useful sentences so the research  actually does support this in some ways  a recent somatic review did find that  the ketogenic diet may actually be  beneficial for patients with autism  however we definitely need a lot more  research and this is another scenario  that you would definitely want to be  monitored by a physician if you're  taking it on especially for children  claim three research flaws in low-fat  diets lately fat has been a really  controversial topic one day it's good  one day it's bad obviously we're always  talking about different kinds of fats  being good or bad and this documentary  really hits this home by criticizing a  lot of the research on the low-fat diet  let's take a look the rising tide of  heart disease and it comes from pretty  much out of nowhere to meet the nation's  leading cause of death in 1955 President  Eisenhower himself has a heart attack  and he's out of the Oval Office for 10  days the nation is fixated on this  problem an urgent public health problem  and nobody really knows what causes  heart disease right there's a number of  different explanations making lack of  vitamins maybe it's car exhaust so into  this vacuum steps Ancel keys a  pathologist from the University of  Minnesota and he says it's saturated  fats saturated fats and cholesterol  cause heart disease the document  criticizes the original research that  suggests that high-fat diets are linked  to heart disease they specifically are  calling out dr. Ansell keys who has  since been criticized and his research  has been called really selective and  biased and flawed several systematic  reviews nowadays have not been able to  show a clear association between total  dietary fat  and heart disease and this documentary  makes the claim that the low-fat diet  may actually do more harm than good  because when we take fat out of our diet  a lot of food manufacturers have to add  in a lot of sugar and salt to make up  for it so I definitely agree with them  on that part that's not necessarily a  great thing the other thing is a lot of  naturally high-fat foods are really  really good for you think about things  like avocado and nuts and seeds so so so  good so while I definitely advocate for  a little bit more balance where carbs  and fats can coexist I don't think that  going completely fat-free is really the  ideal claim number for the keto diet  Kuras diabetes so the documentary talks  a little bit about patient who's able to  completely get off insulin altogether  using the keto diet let's take a look  since the two months that I've been  doing this food program - it may just be  six weeks and within a month we were  able to decrease her insulin by half  even more I don't have anymore  sliding-scale regular short-acting I  have the long-term is down from 50 units  to 20 units a night so according to the  research a low to moderate carb diet may  help in the management of blood glucose  levels however we don't see the same  effect in the long term so it's safe to  say that the keto diet may help in the  management of type 2 diabetes however  whether it cures diabetes or whether it  can completely take you off of insulin  altogether has yet really to be seen in  the literature number 5 the keto diet  helps you lose weight so one of the big  reasons why people go on the keto diet  is of course to lose those big lbs and  the magic pill talks a lot about that so  let's meet